  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    This guide supports shoppers on the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> directory at kakobuy-cn.com. Canada has historically been a "safe haven" for spreadsheet hauls, but 2026 marks a turning point. With the full implementation of the <strong>CARM (Assessment and Revenue Management)</strong> system and the ongoing <strong>Operation Blizzard</strong> crackdown, the Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) has significantly modernized its inspection protocols. For "Maple" shoppers, the days of "declare $14 and forget it" are increasingly risky.
  </p>

  <h2>Operation Blizzard & The National Targeting Centre</h2>
  <p>
    Launched in February 2025 and expanded into 2026, <strong>Operation Blizzard</strong> is a targeted initiative to increase examinations of high-volume e-commerce parcels. Using data from the National Targeting Centre, CBSA now uses AI-driven risk assessments to flag parcels where the <strong>declared value</strong> does not match the <strong>visual density</strong> of the box on an X-ray.
  </p>

  <h2>The CARM System: Digital Oversight</h2>
  <p>
    As of late 2024, the CARM system became the official portal for all Canadian imports. This means your shipping history is now digitally tracked. If you ship 10 parcels a year all declared at exactly $14, the system will eventually flag you for a "valuation audit."
  </p>

  <h2>2026 "Safe Zone" Declaration Strategies</h2>
  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Strategy</th>
