          <th>Best For</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Flat $14 USD</td>
          <td>High (in 2026)</td>
          <td>Small &lt; 2kg parcels</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$10-$12 USD per kg</td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>Standard 5-10kg hauls</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Full Value (DDP)</td>
          <td>Zero</td>
          <td>High-value designer hauls</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Keyword Optimization for CBSA</h2>
  <p>
    CBSA officers look for specific "trigger words." When filling out your Kakobuy declaration, follow these 2026 rules:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Avoid:</strong> "Brand Name," "Luxury," "Gift," or "Sample."</li>
    <li><strong>Use:</strong> "Casual Cotton Apparel," "Unbranded Athletic Shoes," or "Synthetic Outerwear."</li>
    <li><strong>Be Specific:</strong> Instead of "Clothes," use "Men's Woven Trousers (2pcs)."</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Carrier Choice: Canada Post vs. Private Couriers</h2>
  <p>
    In 2026, <strong>EMS (delivered by Canada Post)</strong> remains the safest route. Canada Post handles such massive volumes that manual inspections are still statistically lower than private couriers like UPS or FedEx, who have dedicated brokerage teams looking for duty-eligible parcels.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Summary:</strong> Don't let a lazy declaration kill your haul. Reconcile your spreadsheet picks with a realistic value and follow our <a href="/blog/kakobuy-customs-declared-value-primer/">general customs primer</a> to ensure your paperwork is bulletproof.
  </p>
